## Answer Leak Self-Check

The problem statement contains only the parametric equations of the evolute and the relation $c^2 = a^2 - b^2$. No solution, answer value, proof sketch, or formalization notes are present. No leak detected. Proceeding to solve.

## Solution

We compute the arc length of the evolute of the ellipse $\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1$ (with $a>b>0$), given parametrically by
$$x=\frac{c^2}{a}\cos^3 t,\qquad y=-\frac{c^2}{b}\sin^3 t,\qquad c^2=a^2-b^2.$$

### Step 1. Compute $\dot x$ and $\dot y$

$$\dot x=-\frac{3c^2}{a}\cos^2 t\,\sin t,\qquad \dot y=-\frac{3c^2}{b}\sin^2 t\,\cos t.$$

### Step 2. Form $\dot x^2+\dot y^2$

$$\dot x^2+\dot y^2=\frac{9c^4}{a^2}\cos^4 t\sin^2 t+\frac{9c^4}{b^2}\sin^4 t\cos^2 t
=9c^4\cos^2 t\sin^2 t\left(\frac{\cos^2 t}{a^2}+\frac{\sin^2 t}{b^2}\right).$$

Combining the bracket over a common denominator,
$$\frac{\cos^2 t}{a^2}+\frac{\sin^2 t}{b^2}=\frac{b^2\cos^2 t+a^2\sin^2 t}{a^2b^2}.$$

Hence
$$\dot x^2+\dot y^2=\frac{9c^4\cos^2 t\sin^2 t}{a^2b^2}\bigl(b^2\cos^2 t+a^2\sin^2 t\bigr),$$

and the line element is
$$ds=\frac{3c^2}{ab}\,|\sin t\cos t|\,\sqrt{b^2\cos^2 t+a^2\sin^2 t}\;dt.$$

### Step 3. Exploit symmetry

The evolute is a four-cusped astroid-like curve with cusps at $t=0,\tfrac{\pi}{2},\pi,\tfrac{3\pi}{2}$. By the four-fold symmetry, the total length is four times the length of the arc in the first quadrant ($0\le t\le\tfrac{\pi}{2}$, where $\sin t,\cos t\ge0$):

$$L=4\cdot\frac{3c^2}{ab}\int_0^{\pi/2}\sin t\cos t\,\sqrt{b^2\cos^2 t+a^2\sin^2 t}\;dt.$$

### Step 4. Evaluate the integral by substitution

Set
$$u=b^2\cos^2 t+a^2\sin^2 t.$$

Then
$$du=2(a^2-b^2)\sin t\cos t\,dt=2c^2\sin t\cos t\,dt,$$
so $\sin t\cos t\,dt=\dfrac{du}{2c^2}$. The endpoints are
$$t=0:\ u=b^2,\qquad t=\tfrac{\pi}{2}:\ u=a^2.$$

Therefore
$$\int_0^{\pi/2}\sin t\cos t\,\sqrt{b^2\cos^2 t+a^2\sin^2 t}\;dt
=\int_{b^2}^{a^2}\frac{\sqrt u}{2c^2}\,du
=\frac{1}{2c^2}\cdot\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\Big|_{b^2}^{a^2}
=\frac{a^3-b^3}{3c^2}.$$

### Step 5. Assemble the total length

$$L=4\cdot\frac{3c^2}{ab}\cdot\frac{a^3-b^3}{3c^2}=\frac{4(a^3-b^3)}{ab}.$$

### Final answer

The arc length of the evolute of the ellipse is

$$\boxed{\,L=\dfrac{4(a^3-b^3)}{ab}\,}.$$

(Equivalently, using $a^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2)$ and $c^2=(a-b)(a+b)$, one may write $L=\dfrac{4c^2(a^2+ab+b^2)}{ab(a+b)}$.)

